<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hammurabi of Kurda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of Kurda was a king of Kurda, a small state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, in the 18th century BCE, a contemporary of his more famous namesake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. He is known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> letters, which show him making and breaking alliances with Mari, Andarig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and Babylon during the struggles that followed the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>.</p></div>
